From 5c9bb60c715a76b921304d065cbc6d9f8e1bed03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: idealinsane <idealinsane@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:43:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] executor: bracket programs with the NFS proxy activation
 barrier

knfsd-syzkaller target mode (S1a): the proxy applies its installed rule
set only to NFS calls that start inside an activation window of a KCOV
program generation, and the executor owns that window.

When the lane fixture provides /syz-nfs-lanes/proc-N/activation/ (the
proxy's activation.sock and the installed rule_set_id), the executor
connects to the endpoint before the sandbox chroot.  Only the fork
server keeps that connection, and it alone decides when a program's
window ends: the process that runs fuzzed syscalls has no descriptor
for the endpoint, and nothing it sends can end its window early.

For every execution with a managed remote-coverage generation:

- the program process asks the fork server, over a socket pair made
  for that program, to ACTIVATE its generation before its first call;
  the fork server sends the activation with the SHA-256 of its own copy
  of the exec-encoded program and answers run or skip;
- the program does not run unless the activation was ACKed (or when
  KCOV BEGIN returned no generation), and the KCOV generation is
  aborted instead of published;
- every program process runs in a cgroup v2 directory of its own,
  which is also the root of its cgroup namespace, so everything it
  starts (helpers in their own process group or session included)
  stays in it; with the cgroups feature it is the cgroup programs see
  as ./cgroup, and the fork server stays out of it;
- after its calls the program process sends DONE and waits.  DONE is
  only a request (fuzzed syscalls can send it too): the fork server
  freezes the program cgroup (SIGCONT does not undo that), finishes (or
  aborts) the KCOV generation itself, publishes the generation's remote
  coverage into the program's output, and kills the cgroup;
- the window is closed only once the program cgroup is empty, i.e.
  after every task of the program ran its exit and closed its
  descriptors, so the program's teardown is inside the window too; a
  program that exits, crashes or hangs is handled the same way, before
  the orphan KCOV ABORT, and a cgroup that does not empty leaves the
  window open and the lease uncleared;
- anything but one ACTIVATE and one DONE on the pair leaves the lease
  uncleared.

The barrier outcome is reported in ProgInfo (nfs_proxy), published by
the fork server.  The runner fails, like a hung fixed lane, when the
lease could not be confirmed released or the proxy session cannot open
another generation, so VM recovery recreates the lane.  syz-execprog
prints the barrier and writes it to the coverage .meta file.

Executions without a managed generation (comparisons, machine-check
feature probes without remote coverage) run without a window and so are
never mutated.
